//
// A TEMPLATE MEMBER ALONE DOES NOT SATISFY A BOUND (loft#1826, @FR-G-Sat).  `(G-Sat)` asks
// for a CONCRETE function of the bound's signature, and the monomorph binds the bound's call
// to a definition with no step that would instantiate a template.  A type whose only `OpAdd`
// is `fn OpAdd<U>(self: W, o: U)` satisfied `Addable`, and the template itself reached the
// bytecode: an internal compiler error on both backends.  It is refused naming the template.
// `==` is refused the same way rather than compared by content (G-Sat-Eq), because the type
// HAS an `OpEq`, which the concrete `a == b` instantiates; content in the generic would
// answer differently from the concrete site.  The accepting half is
// `1826-an-operator-reaches-a-template-member-as-its-call-does.loft`.
// @C132 — a user `fn Op…` is no operator template: this file keeps that half of the decision
// today; the rest (a user `fn Op…` binds no operator at all) lands with loft#1833.
//
// @falsified-at: f143377a8 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 1/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 1/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1826b-a-template-member-alone-does-not-satisfy-a-bound.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 1/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 1/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
//   CHANNEL: expect — the control accepts both bounds and crashes in code generation.
//   WITNESS: on the control `plus(a, a)` is an internal compiler error ("a call to the
//   template t_1W_OpAdd reached the bytecode — every call names an instance").
//   HOLDS: leak and panic are equal on both trees.

struct W { x: integer }
fn OpAdd<U>(self: W, _o: U) -> W { W { x: self.x + 1 } }
fn OpEq<U>(self: W, _o: U) -> boolean { self.x == 7 }
fn plus<T: Addable>(a: T, b: T) -> T { a + b }
fn same<T: Equatable>(a: T, b: T) -> boolean { a == b }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: 'W' does not satisfy interface 'Addable': 'OpAdd' is a template, and a bound takes a concrete 'OpAdd' of its signature; declare one for 'W'
// @EXPECT_ERROR: 'W' does not satisfy interface 'Equatable': 'OpEq' is a template, and a bound takes a concrete 'OpEq' of its signature; declare one for 'W'
fn refused() {
  a = W { x: 7 };
  assert(plus(a, a).x == 8 && same(a, a), "unreachable");
}
